    {
      "id": "lpic1-sticky-collaboration",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "LPIC-1",
      "unit": "linux-sticky-directory-policy",
      "topic": "Restricted deletion in shared folders",
      "title": "Keep collaboration while limiting deletion",
      "summary": "Add restricted deletion without locking teammates out or claiming that it protects writable file contents.",
      "objective": "Repair /work/shared so teammates can create items and remove their own names, but cannot remove or rename another teammate’s names. Keep the supplied ordinary directory read/write/search permissions, all file contents, file permissions, ownership and groups unchanged. Retain the directory-owner exception, visitors’ lack of write access and existing file-write behavior.",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "minutes": 18,
      "steps": [
        "Understand the task",
        "Investigate and repair",
        "Verify the complete handover"
      ],
      "guide": [],
      "explanation": "Directory write and search allow changes to names. Restricted deletion adds an ownership check: an ordinary user must own the entry or its containing directory. It does not supply missing directory permissions or stop writes into an existing writable file. The team’s original access stays intact while the sticky flag changes removal and rename behavior.",
      "hints": [
        "Inspect the shared directory with ls -ld and inspect its files with ls -l. File write permission and permission to remove a name are different.",
        "Try the supplied participant actions. Removing group access blocks collaboration; changing a file’s mode does not restrict changes to its name.",
        "The worked example adds the restricted-deletion flag without changing ordinary read/write/search bits. Apply that idea to the assignment directory, then check every outcome."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-101-102-objectives/#rdp-we-104.5_Manage_file_permissions_and_ownership (LPIC-1 v5.0; checked 2026-11-05)",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/unlink.2.html (Linux man-pages 6.19; checked 2026-11-05)",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/rename.2.html (Linux man-pages 6.19; checked 2026-11-05)",
        "https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/Changing-Special-Mode-Bits.html (installed Coreutils 9.10 Info/manual inspected; live web fetch unavailable 2026-11-05)"
      ],
      "simplifications": "One bounded virtual filesystem, fixed directories and ordinary simulated users. Restricted deletion controls removing or replacing names, not writing existing file contents. No root/capability overrides, ACL/SELinux, SUID/SGID, links, real timestamps, creating/removing/moving directories, recursive operations or host commands. Only the disclosed literal command subset is supported. Participant checks execute in discarded simulated copies, never real user accounts.",
