      "id": "lpic1-sticky-collision-handover",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "LPIC-1",
      "unit": "linux-sticky-directory-policy",
      "topic": "Restricted deletion in shared folders",
      "title": "Publish without replacing a colleague’s file",
      "summary": "Diagnose a blocked replacement and complete an owned-file handover without weakening shared-folder policy.",
      "objective": "Publish /work/staging/draft.txt as /work/handover/bea-release.txt with its contents, owner, group and permissions intact. Retire only /work/staging/draft.txt and /work/staging/obsolete.txt. Preserve Chen’s existing /work/handover/release.txt, all other files and both shared directories’ existing access and restricted-deletion policies.",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "minutes": 22,
      "steps": [
        "Understand the task",
        "Investigate and repair",
        "Verify the complete handover"
      ],
      "guide": [],
      "explanation": "A move needs permission to remove the source name and, when the destination exists, to replace that destination name. Bea owns the source but not Chen’s existing release or its directory. A new named destination avoids replacing Chen’s file. Removing only Bea’s obsolete entry completes the handover. Direct writes into an existing writable file remain a separate permission question.",
      "hints": [
        "Use ls -ld on each shared directory and ls -l on the source and existing destination. Who owns each entry?",
        "An existing destination has its own restricted-deletion check. A new destination does not replace another person’s name. Do not weaken the shared directory policy.",
        "Use the named Bea destination, retire only the two specified original names and check the full result. If you overwrite Chen’s contents, the supplied /work/retained-release.txt can restore the existing writable file without removing its name."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-101-102-objectives/#rdp-we-104.5_Manage_file_permissions_and_ownership (LPIC-1 v5.0; checked 2026-11-05)",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/unlink.2.html (Linux man-pages 6.19; checked 2026-11-05)",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/rename.2.html (Linux man-pages 6.19; checked 2026-11-05)",
        "https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/Changing-Special-Mode-Bits.html (installed Coreutils 9.10 Info/manual inspected; live web fetch unavailable 2026-11-05)"
      ],
      "simplifications": "One bounded virtual filesystem, fixed directories and ordinary simulated users. Restricted deletion controls removing or replacing names, not writing existing file contents. No root/capability overrides, ACL/SELinux, SUID/SGID, links, real timestamps, creating/removing/moving directories, recursive operations or host commands. Only the disclosed literal command subset is supported. Participant checks execute in discarded simulated copies, never real user accounts.",
